{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 456 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8a8730bf3c50581903cac55e3e87e9b6d6551e40e521da7e90b53ed1a7c5aaf2" :edb/db-before-t 455 :edb/db-after-t 456 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533768 50 #inst "2026-10-04T21:08:24.022Z" 13194139533768 true] [17592186110593 1020 #edb/ref 17592186249985 13194139533768 true] [17592186110593 1020 #edb/ref 17592186249992 13194139533768 true] [17592186110595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186249978 13194139533768 true] [17592186111036 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533768 true] [17592186111036 1145 "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/b23108c36c016f3022dd3650bbd8bb70ebea27c19f8b8f62d439a4eebe7187f4.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/3dcd10cdeec52258b04705865196a1d59269c298b3821b87f7b0afe7727f740b.png)" 13194139533768 true] [17592186111316 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533768 true] [17592186111316 1145 "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249978 1017 #edb/ref 17592186249979 13194139533768 true] [17592186249978 1089 "q-167338" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249978 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533768 true] [17592186249978 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/ec6deda5b2400258af6ad455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249978 1113 #uuid "8939a741-dcf0-52cf-a781-11f9336a3693" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249978 1145 "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249980 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249981 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249982 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249983 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249984 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1041 "selection" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1104 #edb/ref 17592186249981 13194139533768 true] [17592186249979 1110 #uuid "d94f5858-bbd6-5b39-9230-8b2035d37f39" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249980 1034 "\\frac{28}{5}" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533768 true] [17592186249980 1111 #uuid "fd61aa77-1866-549b-81ab-e873fa601dbd" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249981 1034 "7" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533768 true] [17592186249981 1111 #uuid "1b7e42b1-9d90-5fb1-9a1f-258b07c90f39" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249982 1034 "\\frac{35}{4}" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533768 true] [17592186249982 1111 #uuid "4ac7575e-0550-55af-a433-7b447bde60df" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249983 1034 "\\frac{1}{7}" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533768 true] [17592186249983 1111 #uuid "040a25f0-253a-5aed-876e-037399840c82" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249984 1034 "\\frac{49}{5}" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249984 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533768 true] [17592186249984 1111 #uuid "2b672602-da10-5d3e-bbd5-4af5e71f3202" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249985 1017 #edb/ref 17592186249986 13194139533768 true] [17592186249985 1089 "q-28981" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249985 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533768 true] [17592186249985 1100 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\n- Its range is $y>0$\n- It is an increasing function\n- $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249985 1113 #uuid "dec52dca-c6e8-5d10-98b6-f6a6bc404648" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249985 1145 "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249987 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249988 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249989 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249990 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249991 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1041 "selection" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1104 #edb/ref 17592186249988 13194139533768 true] [17592186249986 1110 #uuid "fe47b545-d0c9-53dd-a1b1-09cea81f1901" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249987 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249987 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249987 1111 #uuid "14888b4c-a03a-5a91-b79f-1edee3a59218" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249988 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249988 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249988 1111 #uuid "c8c363ef-2f3c-5f67-88ba-30824c194a81" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249989 1034 "I only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249989 1111 #uuid "725afeb6-e3bb-5ca4-940a-eac455458a9c" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249990 1034 "III only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249990 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249990 1111 #uuid "6f7ef81e-2059-543d-af83-dfffa4b44570" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249991 1034 "II only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249991 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249991 1111 #uuid "f8b5e499-e673-54e4-81cf-24244582d966" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249992 1017 #edb/ref 17592186249993 13194139533768 true] [17592186249992 1089 "q-47745" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249992 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533768 true] [17592186249992 1100 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}?$\n\n- Its domain is $x>0$\n- Its range is $-\\infty <y<\\infty $\n- It has a horizontal asymptote $y=0$" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249992 1113 #uuid "25c049b0-d191-57a8-a5d5-4a1829c58e6e" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249992 1145 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=(0.125)^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/20fae3a813e249a6423567b448a1f5515c3b9127e7c0b82aad541e720db03aca.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $y=0$ is a horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249994 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249995 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249996 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249997 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186249998 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1041 "selection" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1104 #edb/ref 17592186249997 13194139533768 true] [17592186249993 1110 #uuid "56aa4da9-c9b0-598b-92b5-ccea76c3b639" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249994 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249994 1111 #uuid "aaff22bf-6cfd-56c9-b196-6794367840c3" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249995 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249995 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249995 1111 #uuid "88323acb-113b-5d92-9851-0de21f1a8446" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249996 1034 "I only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249996 1111 #uuid "8f280868-28b0-5807-a0bc-bf255d95d371" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249997 1034 "III only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249997 1111 #uuid "2370c36c-b2b6-52ac-ba2f-905cea931fdd" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249998 1034 "II only" 13194139533768 true] [17592186249998 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533768 true] [17592186249998 1111 #uuid "cee47831-efc3-5955-9a5d-9a0763761256" 13194139533768 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-167338" 1759218624